<span class="eyebrow">FAQ</span>
<h2>What buyers still want to know</h2>
</div>
<div>
<div class="faq-list"><details class="faq-item" open=""><summary>Can a cordless vacuum replace a regular vacuum?</summary><p class="faq-answer">For many apartments, condos, hard-floor homes, and regular maintenance cleaning, yes. In large homes with thick carpet, a cordless vacuum may be better as the everyday cleaner rather than the only vacuum.</p></details><details class="faq-item" open=""><summary>Does suction power matter most in a cordless vacuum?</summary><p class="faq-answer">Not by itself. Suction matters, but floorhead design, airflow, brushroll behavior, filtration, weight, dustbin design, and clog resistance usually matter more in daily ownership.</p></details><details class="faq-item" open=""><summary>How long should a cordless vacuum battery realistically last?</summary><p class="faq-answer">Advertised runtime is usually based on low-power settings. In normal or auto modes, expect less runtime, and remember that battery fade over several years is one of the main ownership risks in this category.</p></details><details class="faq-item" open=""><summary>Are cordless vacuums good for pet hair?</summary><p class="faq-answer">The better ones are, but the label on the box is not enough. Look for consistent hair pickup, low hair wrap, a usable mini motorized tool, and a bin/filter system that does not become messy after repeated pet-hair cleaning.</p></details><details class="faq-item" open=""><summary>Are auto-empty cordless vacuums worth it?</summary><p class="faq-answer">They are worth considering if emptying the bin is the part you hate most, especially with pets, kids, fine dust, or allergy concerns. But they add bulk, bags, noise, cost, and more parts that can fail.</p></details><details class="faq-item"><summary>Why not just buy the newest flagship model?</summary><p class="faq-answer">Because newer does not always mean better ownership. A proven model with strong parts availability, known maintenance patterns, and a lower price can be a smarter decision than the latest high-priced launch.</p></details><details class="faq-item"><summary>What is the biggest mistake people make with cordless vacuums?</summary><p class="faq-answer">They shop like they are buying a motor instead of an ownership product. The regrets usually come from weight, bin emptying, battery life, hair wrap, filters, clogs, and replacement parts.</p></details><details class="faq-item"><summary>How much should most people spend on a cordless vacuum?</summary><p class="faq-answer">Most buyers should avoid the very cheapest end and avoid paying extra for flagship features they will not use. The safest zone is usually mid-to-premium pricing where battery quality, cleaning consistency, and parts support are stronger.</p></details>
</div>
